buildings are surprisingly big in their  impacts all buildings generally  contribute to climate change it turns  out that buildings are responsible for  about 40 percent of the carbon emissions  Living Building Challenge shines a light  on what needs to change  I grew up in northern Canada in a mining  city called Sudbury and Sudbury was  famous for its environmental pollution  we had at the time the world's tallest  smokestack was taller than the Eiffel  Tower and looked like a big cigarette  stuck in the ground and when I was very  little I used to think it was a cloud  making machine until my mother corrected  me that it was a pollution and  cancer-causing machine and that also  inspired me to be an environmentalist at  a young age I became frustrated with I  would call the rate of change in the  building industry I think you know  somewhere in you know 2004-2005 I really  decided that I needed to create a new  framework for the building industry and  began working on it so when somebody  came to our Foundation and asked for a  grant to pursue the Living Building  Challenge as a way of influencing  architecture we not only made them a  grant that we started to investigate  whether we could build a building  ourselves the bullet foundation is is at  its core and environmental philanthropy  and I I genuinely believe that people  who work in a field need to walk their  talk Living Building Challenge is saying  how do we build with materials that are  more responsible how do we not  contribute to climate change it's a very  different way of looking at the role  that buildings play in the environment  we started looking at the office space  that's available in Seattle the building  that we were in was wildly inefficient  and I just felt we needed to move to  someplace that was greener we decided we  would go and build our own building and  explore kind of the frontiers of what  could be done  we set out to build a really super high  performance building and when we did  that we had one main goal in mind and  that was to meet the Living Building  Challenge to be net zero energy net zero  water to make a building that does no  harm to people or the planet so net zero  energy is an interesting concept what it  means is that on an annual basis the  building uses as much electricity as it  generates so we are looking at renewable  sources of energy  and that led us to wind and solar wind  is not an ideal solution in an urban  setting where you have a lot of  buildings and a lot of turbulence solar  was really going to be the ideal  solution and and of course this is  Seattle so we weren't sure how it was  gonna work it was understandable that  there was skepticism when we look at the  average commercial office building in  Seattle they have an energy use index of  something like 72 Killah BTUs per square  foot per year the target for this  building at the end of simulation was 16  a lot of folks justifiably said you know  you can't do that so the main approach  to that is actually by not using lots of  energy in the first place most of the  building is designed around working with  the climate using the Sun using the wind  using the light to create a comfortable  environment and the building is  activated by the weather so when a cloud  goes over the shades go up when the Sun  comes out they go down and in the  building enclosure is almost alive the  biggest challenge with hitting the net  zero energy target was that we couldn't  control what the tenants do if people  within the office choose to bring in a  high energy consumption equipment such  as space heaters it really jeopardizes  meeting their energy budget so there's a  strong incentive to use energy in a  smart way in the end we produced 54  percent more energy than we used last  year the building and its tenants prove  the skeptics wrong with water in the  Living Building Challenge we talked  about being net positive the building  uses rain water for all purposes our  systems are in place to capture  rainwater on the roof when the rain  falls it goes down into a cistern in the  basement of the building and then water  is pulled through a series of filters to  remove any impurities and we're left  with clean potable water the building  actually captures a lot more rainwater  it needs and so you know it's an  opportunity to actually supply other  other buildings with rain water so it's  almost a little municipal water  treatment plant right in the middle of  the city there's something called sick  building syndrome and a lot of office  buildings especially in downtown  locations there  the windows don't open the indoor air  quality can actually be really terrible  and bad for human health and it's  because so many of the materials inside  the building are putting off toxic fumes  productivity has gone up for the people  that move into the building and for sure  Health has theirs let's sick leave here  and finally that a surprise to everyone  people who have seasonal affective  disorder in Seattle people who get  depressed by the near constant cloud  cover see that tend to go away when  they're in the bullet center they're  exposed to so much natural light that  they just don't get that depression  well at the time that the bullet center  was being done it was the biggest living  building here we had we were pushing the  boundaries with the solar array we're  pushing boundaries with the water system  and so there were a lot of hurdles that  we had to get through and those are  things we were we were concerned about  five years ago if you had said we're  gonna have an office building in Seattle  powered entirely by the Sun people might  have thought what are you talking about  can't be done it's Seattle it's cloudy  but we've done it and now we've shown  people that it's possible and I suspect  it within five or ten years we're gonna  see a lot of buildings that are better  than the Bullitt Center in terms of  their use of energy and their beauty and  their ability to provide healthy  environments without leaving an  environmental impact we've toured maybe  eight thousand people through this  building since its opened and that  includes lots and lots of people who  have been inspired by the building and  want to follow this path I hope to  change a lot of things with the creation  of the Living Building Challenge I I  wanted to just shake the building  industry up to challenge them that's why  the word challenge is in the title to  think very differently I want to see new  products that are healthier out in in  the industry I want to see better  buildings that are more energy efficient  water efficient and we've had a lot of  success which is very exciting  you  [Music]
 

